# Proof

## Givens (Evidence)
- evidence_5: Users enter operands via numeric keys and operators via dedicated buttons; multi-digit operands accumulate until an operator is pressed.
- evidence_6: The calculator displays one numeric result at a time on a single-line readout, plus a secondary line for the in-progress expression.
- evidence_7: All operands and intermediate results are IEEE-754 double-precision floating-point numbers.
- evidence_8: Power users requested four memory operations: M+, M-, MR, MC.

## Lemmas (Propositions)
- proposition_15: The calculator supports exactly six binary operations: +, -, *, /, ^, %.
- proposition_16: For any fixed operand sequence and operator sequence, the calculator produces a single deterministic result.
- proposition_17: The memory register persists across AC (all-clear) and is only emptied by MC (memory-clear).

## Theorems (Resolutions)
- resolution_21: When the divisor is zero, display "Error: ÷0" on the readout and require AC before any further input.
